>
  <div class="thoughts-page min-h-screen">
    <article class="essay max-w-[728px] mx-auto px-6 py-16">

      <EssayHeader slug="a-skull-from-georgia" />

      <p class="e-thesis"><span class="e-thesis-label">In short:</span> The common job-application label "White or Caucasian (Not Hispanic or Latino)" is built from a subtraction, a color word, and an eighteenth-century anatomist's compliment to a skull. It has survived fifty years of careful revision of every other category because no one it names has ever complained.</p>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">You have met this line. It sits in a dropdown near the bottom of a job application, fourth or fifth down depending on the vendor, and it reads in full: <strong>White or Caucasian (Not Hispanic or Latino)</strong>. About three in five Americans pick it. Almost nobody reads it. Nothing in it looks like news.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Read it anyway, slowly, as a piece of writing. It has three parts, from three different centuries and three incompatible systems of thought.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>"Not Hispanic or Latino"</strong> is a subtraction, and what it subtracts is a category that did not exist before 1977.</li>
          <li><strong>"White"</strong> is a color word doing duty as a proper noun, describing a population whose actual skin tones run most of the available range.</li>
          <li><strong>"Caucasian"</strong> is a mountain range. Behind the mountain range is a particular dead woman's skull, and behind the skull is an aesthetic judgment made in 1795 by a German anatomist about which of the skulls in his cabinet he found the most beautiful.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>That is the whole label: a negation, a color, and a compliment paid to a corpse. It is the only entry on the form assembled that way, it is the largest entry, and it is the only one left substantially alone through fifty years of fastidious revision of everything around it. Those three facts are really one fact, and it is not about race. It is about who has to be listened to.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="my-14">
        <img
          src="https://art.jamestannahill.com/weather/2026-08-12-060044/region-30n-60w/preview-1200.webp"
          width="1024"
          height="1024"
          alt="Hard-edged abstract painting of overlapping circles in red, orange, blue, yellow and black crossed by fine ruled lines on a cream ground"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
          class="w-full h-auto t-plate"
        />
        <figcaption class="e-cap mt-3">
          30N 60W, western North Atlantic, rendered from that day's atmospheric data.
          From <a href="https://art.jamestannahill.com/weather/2026-08-12-060044/region-30n-60w/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="underline hover:opacity-70 break-all">art.jamestannahill.com/weather/2026-08-12-060044/region-30n-60w</a>.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <h2 class="e-h2">The Prettiest One in the Cabinet</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">Johann Friedrich Blumenbach taught medicine at Göttingen and collected skulls, some eighty of them by 1795, acquired through the ordinary channels of eighteenth-century natural history: soldiers, physicians, travelers and the Russian court. In the third edition of his treatise on the natural varieties of mankind, published in 1795, he settled on five varieties. For the one that included Europeans he coined a word that had not previously been used in this sense. He called them Caucasian.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">He explained why, and that part did not survive into the dropdown. He had a skull from a Georgian woman, and he thought it the most beautiful specimen he owned. On the strength of that beauty he decided the Caucasus was probably where the human species had started and that everyone else had departed from it. So he named the variety after the mountains. That is the heart of the argument. It was not measurement, lineage, or anything a modern person would call evidence. It was a judgment of taste, promoted to a claim about origins, then fixed as a name on several hundred million people.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Stephen Jay Gould made this point in <em>The Geometer of Race</em>, along with a second point that is usually dropped when the first is quoted. Blumenbach was, for his time, a decent and generous man. He insisted the varieties shaded continuously into one another with no sharp boundary anywhere. He wrote against the idea that Africans were intellectually inferior and collected their published works to prove it. He would have been appalled by most of what the next century did with his vocabulary. His sin was not malice. It was the smaller and more durable one of arranging a continuum around a favorite, so that everything else became a degree of departure from it. Once you have a center you have a periphery, and the periphery is where the nineteenth century went to work.</p>
        <p>There is no version of this history in which "Caucasian" is a technical term. It was never a measurement. The skull-measuring science that came later, craniometry, did claim to measure, and it is the body of work that has been discarded in its entirety, including by the people who were once its keenest practitioners. Strip out the parts that were disproved and the parts that were abandoned, and here is the residue: a word meaning "resembling a skull an anatomist thought was pretty," named for a mountain range where almost none of the people who tick the box have ever lived. Three in five Americans certify themselves against it before lunch.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">The Words That Were Removed</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">The first hypocrisy needs no theory at all. It is a comparison of two lists. American officialdom has spent fifty years removing exactly this kind of language, category by category, and has generally been right to.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The Census retired the word "Negro" from its forms after the 2010 count, on the reasonable ground that the described population had stopped using it about themselves two generations earlier.</li>
          <li>In 2016 a statute struck "Oriental" and "Negro" from two federal statutes, replacing them with terms people actually use.</li>
          <li>The category once labeled "Asian or Pacific Islander" was split in two, because the Pacific Islander population objected that being averaged into a much larger group made them statistically invisible.</li>
          <li>"American Indian" acquired "Alaska Native."</li>
          <li>"Hispanic" acquired "or Latino," and in a great many private systems acquired "or Latinx" as well.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mb-6">Every one of these changes came from the same argument, made by the people described: the word is an artifact of somebody else's taxonomy, we did not choose it, and we would like a different one.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Now set that record of scrupulousness against one fact. The founding coinage of racial typology is still in use on tens of thousands of application forms in 2026, attached to the largest box with the word "or."</p>
        <p class="mb-6">It is not there because anyone defends it. No geneticist uses it. No anthropologist has used it approvingly in fifty years. No ordinary person says it about themselves without irony, and nobody at a party announces that they are Caucasian. The word survives in exactly two places: the police blotter, where it describes a suspect at a distance, and the job application, where it asks you to describe yourself the way the blotter would. In the first, a stranger is guessing at you from across the street. In the second, you have been handed the stranger's vocabulary and asked to do the guessing on his behalf, unpaid, while hoping to be hired.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">To be fair to the federal government, its form does not say "Caucasian" and never has. The 1977 standard said "White," and the 2024 revision still says "White." The word is an in-house addition by employers and vendors somewhere in the drafting, and it has spread by copying. Nobody wrote it. Nobody adopted it. Nobody ever approved it in a meeting. It got into your employer's system the way a virus gets into a school, and it stays for one reason. The only force that has ever removed a word from these forms is a complaint from the people the word is about, and this box does not complain.</p>
        <p>That tells you what the fifty years of careful revision were really made of. It was not a growing scruple about naming people badly. It was a queue. Every correction was earned by somebody organizing, drafting, testifying and waiting, and every correction stopped at the edge of the group that fought for it. Where nobody was pushing, the eighteenth century simply stayed on the page, in bold, in use, untouched. If you want to know whether an institution has a principle or a complaints department, find a place where a principle would apply and no one is complaining, and look at what is sitting there.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="my-14">
        <img
