      <h2 class="e-h2">Defined by Subtraction</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">The front half of the phrase is stranger than the back half, and much less remarked on. Every other line on the form is defined positively. "Black or African American" names an origin. "Asian" names an origin. "American Indian or Alaska Native" names an origin, and in the more careful forms names a tribal enrollment behind it. "Non-Hispanic White" names an origin and then removes part of it. Alone among the categories, it is defined partly by what its members are not. And the thing they are not is a classification that was manufactured, by legislation in 1976 and an administrative directive the following year, out of populations that had not previously regarded themselves as a common kind.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">The arithmetic runs like this. In 1977 the government invents "Hispanic." Having invented it, it must then subtract it from the other categories, because the new category was defined as an ethnicity that cuts across the race question rather than sitting inside it. The subtraction lands hardest on "White," since that is where most of the overlap is. And the residue of that subtraction, a remainder produced by an operation performed on a category that did not exist a decade earlier, is the box that three in five Americans now tick. It is also the reference group in essentially every published statistic about the country.</p>
        <p>A category that is a remainder is not, for that reason, empty. But it is worth being honest about what it is doing. When a statistical analysis reports an outcome for non-Hispanic Whites, that number is not a measurement of a people. It measures whoever is left after two prior definitional decisions have been applied, both made by committee, one of them in living memory, and either of which could be revised on a Tuesday. Being the remainder also has a specific consequence that being a category does not. The remainder is the unmarked case. It is what the other numbers are differences from, which means it never has to be argued for, never gets audited, and never appears in the sentence at all. It is the zero on the ruler, and nobody inspects the zero.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">What the Box Is Wired To</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">A reasonable person might say: fine, the word is silly, the boundary is arbitrary, and none of it matters, because the answer goes into a filing cabinet and no one acts on it. Here is the direct reply. Categories built on exactly this footing, self-reported, without criteria, drawn by committee, have been wired into decisions with bodies on the other end. They were still wired in within the last five years.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>Kidneys.</strong> A blood test yields a number, the estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR), and that number decides whether you are sent to a kidney specialist, whether you qualify for a transplant list, and where on it your name goes. For twenty years the standard equations adjusted that number upward for any patient recorded as Black. The adjustment was not for a measured biological variable. It was for the census box. Adjust the number upward and the patient looks healthier than they are, so the referral comes later. A national task force convened by the two main kidney societies concluded in 2021 that the adjustment should go, and it went. In 2023 the organ allocation system began adjusting the waiting times of Black candidates who had been held back by the old equation. A category with no criterion, entered on a form, multiplied by a number, subtracted from someone's place in the queue for an organ, for twenty years, and then corrected after the fact.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>Other cases.</strong> This is not an isolated instance.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Lung function testing carried race-specific reference equations on the same logic, so a given measured lung capacity was scored against a different expected normal depending on the box. This affected disability determinations and workplace exposure claims until the American Thoracic Society moved to race-neutral equations in 2023.</li>
          <li>In 2005 the Food and Drug Administration approved a heart failure drug with a label restricted to self-identified Black patients, the first race-labeled drug in the country, on trial evidence that did not include a comparison group of anyone else.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mb-6">The input was the same every time: a self-reported administrative category descended from the 1977 directive. The conclusion was the same every time, eventually, in public, from the professional bodies that had built on it. The variable was standing in for something else. It stood in for exposure, income, insurance, and the accumulated effects of being treated as a member of the category. It was never measuring a kind of body. It was measuring a history of treatment, badly, while being read as biology.</p>
        <p>Notice where the "White" box sits in all of that. In every one of those equations, the adjustment was applied to somebody else, which means the box under discussion here was the reference value: the normal that the correction was defined against. A word coined for a skull an anatomist admired became the center of a continuum. Two centuries later, in a clinic, the numbers were still being written as departures from that center. Blumenbach at least said plainly why he had arranged the cabinet that way.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">Twice, in a Single Term</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">The Supreme Court settled whether the boundary is drawn or found, twice, in a single term, three months apart. It contradicted itself so plainly that the second opinion has to spend several pages explaining why the first one does not count.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">In 1922, in <em>Ozawa</em>, a Japanese applicant for naturalization argued that he was white, on grounds including that his skin was in fact paler than that of many Europeans. The Court held that "white" meant "Caucasian," that "Caucasian" was a matter for scientific authority, and that a Japanese man was not one. The reasoning is explicitly typological. The statute is read through Blumenbach's vocabulary, and the vocabulary decides.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Three months later, in <em>Thind</em>, a Punjabi applicant made the obvious move. The anthropology of the day classified high-caste North Indians as Aryan and therefore Caucasian. By the Court's own stated test, he was exactly what it had said was required. The Court held that he was not white. It held that the word in the statute must be read according to the understanding of the common man, that scientific classification was not controlling after all, and that whatever the anthropologists said, ordinary people did not regard this applicant as one of them.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Two rulers giving different lengths, again, and this time in the same courtroom in the same term. Look at what the second opinion had to do. It did not distinguish the first case. It did not refine it. It threw the test away, three months after Justice Sutherland, for a unanimous Court, had announced it, because the test had let in the wrong man. What replaced it was the understanding of the common man, which is to say whatever the answer needed to be. The category has no criterion. It has a job, which is sorting admissions, and it will wear whichever criterion gets the sorting done, dropping the science the instant the science lets the wrong person in. Blumenbach's word was never the reason for anything. It was a coat of paint, and in 1923 it came off in a single term, in public, in the United States Reports.</p>
        <p>And the boundary kept moving after the courts stopped writing about it. The 1977 directive defined "White" as having origins in the original peoples of Europe, North Africa or the Middle East. So for forty-seven years an Egyptian, a Lebanese and a Persian were officially White in the United States, whatever anyone in the building thought when they walked in. In March 2024 the standard was revised, a Middle Eastern or North African category was created, and several million people ceased to be White by memorandum, having done nothing whatever. That is a bird promoted to full species while asleep, with the difference, as ever, that these people can read the notice.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">The Load-Bearing "Or"</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">This raises a question the label invites and has never had to answer. Are the two terms, White and Caucasian, mutually exclusive, identical in scope, or neither? The word joining them makes a promise: that they pick out the same set, and the second is a courtesy to readers who prefer it. The promise fails in both directions at once.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">There are exactly two ways to pry apart an "or": find something on the left that is not on the right, and something on the right that is not on the left. The two cases just described are exactly those. Thind was Caucasian and not white. Ozawa was paler than most Europeans and not Caucasian. Both lost, and that is the finding. Whichever half of the phrase you satisfy, the other half remains available to refuse you. It is not a choice between alternatives. It is an "and" wearing an "or," and the second term exists to be produced when the first has gone the wrong way.</p>
        <p>The front of the label misbehaves in the opposite manner, and more quietly, because it excludes people who are inside it. For most of the period since 1977, race and ethnicity have been asked as two separate questions, deliberately independent, so a person may be Hispanic and of any race. Millions of Americans accordingly answer "White" to the race question and "Hispanic" to the ethnicity question, both truthfully, both by the form's own rules. The compound label then removes them. "Non-Hispanic White" does not name the White population. It names the White population minus some of the White people, subtracted along an axis the form has just finished insisting is a different axis. A set that deletes its own members using a criterion it has declared independent is not a category in any sense a logician would recognize. It is a bookkeeping convenience that has been read aloud so often it now sounds like a description of persons.</p>
      </div>
      <h3 class="e-h3">Can you be wrong about yourself?</h3>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">Now the sharper half: can you deny yourself one of these categories and be wrong? The apparatus answers no, loudly, on every page. You are the authority on what you are, and the institution is only writing it down.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">The instructions answer yes. EEO-1 guidance (the federal workforce-reporting rules) calls self-identification the preferred method, then turns to what happens if the employee declines. The employer files anyway. It may rely on employment records, or on "observer identification." If you decline, someone in the building looks at you and types the answer in. Blumenbach at least had the specimen in front of him and wrote down his reasoning. The observer has a glance across an open-plan floor, a filing deadline, and no obligation to tell you what he decided. Refusal does not leave the field empty. It leaves a blank that the institution is instructed to fill in by eye.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">So the answer is worse than either yes or no. You cannot be wrong, because there is no fact of the matter to be wrong about, and you can be overruled, because there is a filing deadline. The category is both unfalsifiable and involuntary. Deny it and a stranger's glance corrects you. Assert it and no one checks, because there is nothing to check against. Both are the behavior of a category that is about how you are seen, dressed in the language of a category about what you are.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">You can also be wrong in both directions at once. People who have lived near the boundary already know this, and everyone else finds it hard to believe. A person with one grandparent from somewhere is told by one audience that they are claiming something that is not theirs, and by another that they are disowning something that is. These happen on identical facts, in the same week, each correction delivered with the flat confidence of someone pointing out a typo. Both cannot be right. Both can easily be wrong. The only way to make sense of a claim that can fail in opposite directions at once is to notice that it was never a claim about the person. It was a claim about the audience, and the audience changes rooms.</p>
        <p>And keep in mind where this is being asked. It is not a census form from a statistical agency with no view about you. It is not a clinic intake sheet, backed by a duty of care. It is a job application. The party asking is the party deciding whether you eat, and it asks in the same session, in the same interface, ten fields after your salary expectation. It presents a menu you did not write, containing an "or" that has never once been resolved in favor of the applicant, and a subtraction performed on an axis it has just told you is unrelated. It asks you to certify yourself against that string. It tells you the answer is voluntary and that no one will see it, while its own reporting instructions allow that if you decline, somebody there may look at you and write one in. There is no version of that transaction in which the applicant is the authority on anything. The only party in the room with a settled view of what you are is the one holding the job.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">Resolution Follows Politics</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">The second hypocrisy is about how much detail the form collects, and where. A good modern form asks Asian applicants to specify and offers Chinese, Filipino, Asian Indian, Vietnamese, Korean, Japanese and a write-in. It separates Native Hawaiian from Samoan from Chamorro. It asks American Indian respondents to name their enrolled or principal tribe. This is right, and it is right for a reason that was argued for and won: lumping groups together destroys information, and in each case the destroyed information mattered to someone's understanding of their own position.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Then the same form reaches fifty-eight percent of the country. Sicilians. Swedes. Bosnians. Irish Catholics. Ashkenazi Jews. Eleventh-generation Yankees. Different centuries of arrival, different treatment under the very statutes at issue, and in several cases a living memory of not having counted as White at all. One line. Next to a mountain range in the Caucasus.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Notice the shape of the rule. The form's level of detail is highest exactly where the political stakes are highest and lowest where they are lowest. That is not a fact about the population. Nothing about Poles and Portuguese makes them more alike than Koreans and Cambodians. It is a fact about which groups have organized to demand a line of their own. That is a perfectly legitimate way for a democracy to allocate attention, and it should simply be said out loud rather than presented as a taxonomy. A classification whose level of detail is set by lobbying is a record of lobbying.</p>
        <p>In fairness, the 2024 revision addresses this on paper. It requires detailed collection for every category, including White, with examples such as German, Irish, English, Italian and Polish, and it applies the same requirement to the new Middle Eastern or North African line. Whether it survives contact with the software vendors is the open question. The honest early answer is that the applicant tracking system in front of you this afternoon almost certainly still shows one line, and that line very possibly still says "Caucasian."</p>
      </div>
