      <h2 class="e-h2">Who Is Being Inconsistent</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">It would be convenient to load all of this onto one faction, and the convenience is exactly why it should be resisted. The contradictions are distributed with unusual evenness.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>The progressive version.</strong> The position that race is a social construction, historically produced and biologically incoherent, is correct and well supported. It is held with real conviction by a great many people, who then tick a box containing the single most explicitly biological, most explicitly typological word left anywhere in the language, and feel nothing. A culture able to run a two-year consultation on whether to write "Latino" or "Latinx" has walked past a skull-based race term, in bold, in the same dropdown, every working day for fifty years. It was not overlooked. Nothing on that page is overlooked. It simply was not worth anyone's afternoon, because no one named by it was ever going to file a complaint, and the whole machine runs on complaints.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>The conservative version,</strong> which is no better and in one respect worse. The position that Americans should be treated as individuals rather than as members of statistical kinds is serious, and seriously held. It is also held by people who will write "Caucasian" on an incident report without a flicker, who reach for the categories with enthusiasm the moment the subject is crime statistics, and who locate their objection to racial classification at the exact moment the classification is used to count something about them. A principle that switches on and off according to who comes out ahead is not a principle. It is a preference with a lawyer.</p>
        <p>This produces the deepest of the ironies and the quietest. The whole apparatus rests on self-identification: you are the authority on what you are, and the institution is merely writing it down. Yet the answer offered to the largest group in the country is a word supplied by a foreign anatomist about a skull he liked, that no member of that group has ever said aloud about themselves, and that can be overwritten by a stranger's glance if you decline it. It is self-identification, from a menu you did not write, in a vocabulary nobody living speaks, revocable by the party you are asking for a job.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">Where This Breaks</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">The obvious objection is strong, and anyone making this argument who does not concede it plainly is playing a different game than the one advertised. Incoherent at the edges is not the same as meaningless at the center. The American racial system was never a description of biology in the first place. It was an allocation of standing, enforced by statute, property deed restrictions, lending maps and mobs, and it operated on perception rather than definition. So a perception-based category, self-reported and fuzzy at the margin, is not a defect of the instrument. It is a reasonably faithful copy of the thing being measured. The federal housing agencies did not need a coherent theory of ancestry to draw the red line, and the fact that Blumenbach's word is nonsense does not refund a single mortgage. If you want to know whether a neighborhood was excluded, whether a workforce looks like its labor market, or whether a lending pattern is what the bank says it is, you need a category that tracks how people were and are seen. This one, for all its provenance, does.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">There is a further point that cuts the same way. The fluidity of the boundary is often offered as a knockdown argument, as if the movement of the line over time showed that nothing was ever inside it. It shows the opposite. The Irish and the Italians and the Jews were absorbed into the category because the category conferred something worth having. The direction of the movement, always inward and always toward advantage, is itself evidence that the sorting was real. A line that people spend a century trying to cross is not imaginary merely because it moved.</p>
        <p>So the argument here is not that the box should be abolished, and a reader hoping for that will have to look elsewhere. The distinction it turns on is the one the kidney equation makes obvious. Counting a population and defining a person are different operations, and the same category can be adequate for the first and disqualifying for the second. Use it to ask whether a bank lent evenly across a city, and it is doing what it is good for: measuring how people were treated, in aggregate, after the fact. Use it as a variable inside a decision about an individual, whether that decision is a filtration rate, a lung capacity or a hire, and you have taken a term with no criterion behind it and given it a vote. Everything worth changing here follows from keeping those two uses apart.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">Delete One Word</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">Three things follow. They are deliberately the size of things a single person with access to the system could do on a Thursday.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>1. Delete the word "Caucasian."</strong> It is not in the federal standard, so no filing depends on it. It is not a term of art, so no analysis depends on it. It has no constituency, so no one is offended by its removal. It is eighteenth-century race science sitting unattended in a payroll vendor's dropdown, and it costs one line of configuration to remove. It has survived every diversity review, every legal sign-off, every vendor migration and every redesign of that page since 1977. Why? Because nobody in the chain was ever going to be embarrassed in public about it. That is the whole mechanism. Not ideology. Nobody watching.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>2. Move the question to after the offer.</strong> The count is identical, since the people who matter to the count are the people you hired, and the entire cost of asking early falls on the several thousand applicants who were never going to work there. If an employer also wants the top of the funnel, it should have to say out loud that it is buying a denominator for a slide, and weigh the purchase against what it is asking of the person buying it for them.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>3. Say what happens when someone declines.</strong> If the reporting instructions allow an observer to supply the answer, the form should say so, in the same size type as the promise that the question is voluntary. This is the change with the sharpest edge, because no employer will want to print it, and the reluctance is the argument. An institution unwilling to describe its own procedure to the person it is performing the procedure on has told you what the procedure is.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Behind all three is one observation, and the composition of the label is not an accident. A color word is what strangers call you at a distance. A subtraction is what a bureaucracy does to you when it has invented a category and needs somewhere to put the remainder. A mountain range is what a scholar calls you when he has never met you and is working from a specimen. Every component is a description written from outside, by someone with a purpose of their own, and the compound is then handed back to you with the instruction that you identify yourself.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Nobody in the country is named by that string. Nobody thinks in it. What gets recorded when someone selects it is not an identity and not a fact about ancestry. It is compliance with a menu, filed by a person who wanted a job, in a vocabulary chosen for them by a man who never met them, on a page that promises them the opposite.</p>
        <p>Blumenbach kept his skulls in a cabinet and showed them to visitors, and he thought the Georgian one the jewel of the collection, the standard against which the rest could be arranged. He was wrong in the specific way careful men are wrong, which is that he mistook a preference for a finding. Two and a quarter centuries on, the preference has outlived the anatomy, the treatise, the cabinet and the entire science it belonged to. It is still in service. It is working right now, in a payroll vendor's dropdown, in front of a stranger who needs the money. And if that stranger declines to answer it, somebody will look up and answer it for him.</p>
      </div>
